          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em quiver
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gabriel72"}> Gabriel 72
            ) is a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/edges"} edges
            which may stretch between (ordered)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
            of “points”, called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
          %p
            The term “quiver” (German:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em Köcher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
            is (and was) known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graphs"} directed graphs
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+graph"} directed
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudograph"} pseudo
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multigraphs"} multigraphs
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gabriel72"} Gabriel 1972
          %blockquote
            %p
              For such a 4-tuple
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big[
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %munderover
                      %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi t
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi s
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \underoverset{\underset{t}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{s}{\longrightarrow}}{} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big]
              we propose the term
              = succeed "," do
                %em quiver
              = succeed "," do
                %em graph
          %p#ConceptWithAnAttitude
            Hence the notion “quiver” is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concept+with+an+attitude"} concept with an attitude
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
            typically cares about: Namely one is interested in their
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representations"} quiver representations
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#DerksenWeyman05"} Derksen &amp; Weyman 2005, ftn 1
          %blockquote
            %p The underlying motivations of quiver theory are quite different from those in the traditional graph theory. To emphasize this distinction, it is common in our context to use the word “quivers” instead of “graphs”.
          %p
            A key result in
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation"} quiver representation
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%27s+theorem"} Gabriel's theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Gabriel72"} Gabriel 1972
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#slick_definition Slick definition
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} walking
              quiver
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                one
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em vertices
            %li
              %p
                one object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_1
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em edges
            %li
              %p
                two
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo ,
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t\colon X_1 \to X_0
                %em source
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em target
            %li
              %p
                together with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphisms"} identity morphisms
          %p
            A
            %strong quiver
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X \to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            More generally, a
            %strong
              quiver
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
              a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X \to C
          %p
            The category of quivers in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} Quiv
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{X}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects are functors
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon X \to C
            %li
              %p
                morphisms are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformations
                between such functors.
          %p
            In the basic case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaves"} presheaves
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} Quiv
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
          %h3#nutsandbolts_definitions Nuts-and-bolts definitions
          %p
            A
            %strong quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            consists of two sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            (the set of
            %em edges
            of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            (the set of
            %em vertices
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t\colon E \rightrightarrows V
          %p
            (the source and target functions). More generally, a
            %strong quiver internal to a category
            (more simply,
            %em in
            a category)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of two objects
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and two morphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t\colon E \rightrightarrows V
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = (E, V, s, t)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G' = (E', V', s', t')
            are two quivers in a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %strong morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon G \to G'
            is a pair of morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_0\colon V \to V'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_1\colon E \to E'
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s' \circ g_1 = g_0 \circ s
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t' \circ g_1 = g_0 \circ t
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph+theory"} graph theory
            %a{:href => "#DG2nd"} p. 4
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#White84"} Figure 2-2
            %strong directed pseudograph
            (or some variation on that theme), but category theorists often just call them
            = succeed "." do
              %strong directed graphs
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_0 = G(X_0)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1 = G(X_1)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A quiver in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{op}
                with values in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              %p
                A quiver is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+set"} globular set
                which is concentrated in the first two degrees.
            %li
              %p
                A quiver can have distinct edges
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e,e'\in G_1
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi e
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(e) = s(e')
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(e) = t(e')
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(e) = t(e)
            %li
              %p
                A quiver is
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+graph"} complete
                if for any pair of vertices
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mi v
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi G
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v,v'\in G_0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in G_1
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi v
                        %mo ,
                        %mi t
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi e
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi v
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(e) = v, t(e) = v'
          %h2#terminology Terminology
          %p
            Saying
            %em quiver
            instead of
            %em directed (multi)graph
            indicates focus on a certain set of operation intended on that graph. Notably there is the notion of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation"} quiver representation
          %p
            Now, one sees that a
            %em representation
            of a graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in the sense of quiver representation is nothing but a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Q
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho\colon Q := F(G) \to Vect
            from the
            %em free category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(G)
            on the quiver
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Given a graph
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with collection of vertices
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_0
            and collection of edges
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(G)
            on the graph whose collection of objects coincides with the collection of vertices, and whose collection of morphisms consists of finite
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence"} sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that fit together head-to-tail (also known as
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path"}> path
                s
          %p
            Here we are taking advantage of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjunction
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            (the category of small categories) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quiv"} Quiv
            (the category of directed graphs). Namely, any category has an underlying directed graph:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %mi Quiv
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\colon Cat \to Quiv
          %p and the left adjoint of this functor gives the free category on a directed graph:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon Quiv\to Cat
          %p
            Since this is the central operation on quivers that justifies their distinction from the plain concept of directed graph, we may adopt here the point of view that
            %em quiver
            is synonymous with
            = succeed "." do
              %em free category
          %p
            So a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            of a quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q = F(G)
            is a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Q
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\colon Q \to Vect
          %p
            Concretely, such a thing assigns a vector space to each vertex of the graph
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_free_categories Relation to free categories
          %p
            It may be handy to
            %em identify
            a quiver with its free category. This can be justified in the sense that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Quiv
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon Quiv \to Cat
            is an embedding (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-surjectivity"} surjective
            for all
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \gt 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cores"} cores
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            between quivers may be identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalences
            between free categories with no ambiguity.
          %p
            However, at the level of noninvertible morphisms, this doesn't work; while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faithful+functor"} faithful
            %em not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+functor"} full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            between free categories that are not morphisms of quivers.
          %p
            Nevertheless, if we fix a quiver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and a category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation"} representation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is precisely a functor from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(G)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            (or equivalently a quiver morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(D)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphism"} heteromorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is not itself a free category, this is unlikely to cause confusion.
          %h3#relation_to_representation_theory_of_algebras Relation to representation theory of algebras
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            a quiver, write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k Q
            for the
            %em path algebra
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            over a ground field
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k Q
            is an algebra with
            = succeed "-basis" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %p
            A module over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k Q
            is the same thing as a representation of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is acyclic, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k Q
            is finite-dimensional as a vector space, so in studying representations of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
          %h3#Classification Classification
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%27s+theorem"} Gabriel's theorem
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gabriel72"}> Gabriel 72
            ) says that connected quivers with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indecomposable+object"} indecomposable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representations"} quiver representations
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            are precisely the
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+quivers"} Dynkin quivers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/undirected+graph"} undirected graph
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dynkin+diagram"} Dynkin diagram
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ADE+classification"} ADE series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indecomposable+object"} indecomposable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representations"} quiver representations
            are in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            with the positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root+%28in+representation+theory%29"} roots
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/root+system"} root system
            of the Dynkin diagram. (
            %a{:href => "#Gabriel72"}> Gabriel 72
            ).
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %h3#enriched_quivers Enriched quivers
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (or a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is a
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              \-enriched quiver
            if it has a collection of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ob(Q)
            and a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mor
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mor: Ob(Q) \times Ob(Q) \to V
            for all objects
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in Ob(Q)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} loop directed graphs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} endorelations
            are quivers enriched in the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-quiver"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              \-quivers
            are quivers enriched in the category of
            = succeed "-quivers." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} universes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
            is a quiver type enriched in a universe
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Type"} Type
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation"} quiver representation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+quiver"}> McKay quiver
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/McKay+correspondence"} McKay correspondence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+gauge+theory"} quiver gauge theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots-quivers+correspondence"} knots-quivers correspondence
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag+persistence"} zigzag persistence
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebraic
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oidification"} oidification
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magma"} magma
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magmoid"} magmoid
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+set"} pointed
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magma"} magma
                    with an
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunction"} endofunction
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/setoid"}> setoid
                    \/
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bishop+set"} Bishop set
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+magma"} unital magma
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital+magmoid"} unital magmoid
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasigroup"} quasigroup
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasigroupoid"} quasigroupoid
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+%28algebra%29"} loop
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loopoid"} loopoid
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroup"} semigroup
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicategory"} semicategory
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-involution"} anti-involutive
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+category"} dagger category
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+quasigroup"} associative quasigroup
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
