          %h2#references References
          %p
            The concept of quiver in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver+representation"} quiver representation
            (and their classification in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel%27s+theorem"} Gabriel's theorem
          %ul
            %li#Gabriel72
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Gabriel"}> Peter Gabriel
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Unzerlegbare Darstellungen. I
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01298413"} doi:10.1007/BF01298413
              %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=332887"} MR332887
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01298413"} doi
          %p Some general-purpose references include
          %ul
            %li#DerksenWeyman05
              %p
                Harm Derksen, Jerzy Weyman,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quiver representations
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/200502/fea-weyman.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/notices/200502/200502FullIssue.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                William Crawley-Boevey,
                %em Lectures on quiver representations
                (
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-bielefeld.de/~wcrawley/quivlecs.pdf"}> pdf
                ).
            %li
              %p
                Alistair Savage,
                %em Finite-dimensional algebras and quivers
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.arxiv.org/abs/math/0505082"}> arXiv:math/0505082
                ),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Encyclopedia of Mathematical Physics
          %p
            Quivers (referred to as
            = succeed ")" do
              %em directed pseudographs
          %ul
            %li#Ringel1974
              Gerhard Ringel:
              = succeed "." do
                %em Map Color Theorem
          %p Beware that, strictly speaking, for Ringel, “quiver” means “embedded quiver” (into a given surface); in particular the author distinguishes between the two possible orientations of an embedded loop.
          %p Quivers embedded in surfaces are studied in:
          %ul
            %li#White84
              Arthur T. White:
              = succeed "." do
                %em Graphs, Groups and Surfaces
          %p A special kind of quiver (finite, no loops, no parallel arcs) is treated in
          %ul
            %li#DG2nd
              %p
                Gregory Gutin, Jørgen Bang-Jensen:
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Digraphs: Theory, Algorithms and Applications
            %li#GeneralizedGraphs
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Qualitative Distinctions Between Some Toposes of Generalized Graphs
          %p
            Some introductory material on the relation between quivers (there called
            = succeed ")" do
              %em multigraphs
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Perrone"}> Paolo Perrone
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em Notes on Category Theory with examples from basic mathematics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10642"} arXiv
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  Also called “the elementary ”parallel process“ ” by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} Lawvere
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#GeneralizedGraphs"} p. 272
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} Lawvere
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Como"} Como
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
